<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nikolai Rimsky-Korsakov</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1844">1844</a>–<a href="/years/1908">1908</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Nikolai Andreyevich Rimsky-Korsakov</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nikolai Rimsky-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korsakov-sakhalin">Korsakov</a> was a Russian composer and a member of the group known as The Five, whose orchestral works Capriccio Espagnol, the Russian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a> Festival Overture and Scheherazade remain staples of the classical repertoire, along with parts of his fifteen operas. He served as an officer in the Imperial Russian Navy and later as Inspector of Naval Bands. From 1871 he was professor at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> Conservatory, where he taught himself Western methods and trained a generation of composers. He prepared works by The Five, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modest-mussorgsky">Modest Mussorgsky</a>'s, for performance, and his style influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurice-ravel">Maurice Ravel</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claude-debussy">Claude Debussy</a> and Ottorino Respighi.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nikolai-rimsky-korsakov.jpg" alt="Nikolai Rimsky-Korsakov" width="330" height="373" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Valentin Serov · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rimsky-Korsakov_Serow_crop.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nikolai Andreyevich Rimsky-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korsakov-sakhalin">Korsakov</a> was born on 18 March 1844 in Tikhvin, in Novgorod Governorate, into a Russian noble family whose members had served as governors and generals. He developed a passion for the sea in childhood from books and from his older brother's naval service, and became an officer in the Imperial Russian Navy. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> in May 1865 he did little music until contact with Mily Balakirev in September 1865 drew him back; at Balakirev's suggestion he wrote a trio for the scherzo of the symphony in E♭ minor and reorchestrated it, and Balakirev conducted its first performance that December.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1871, at 27, he became Professor of Practical Composition and Instrumentation at the Saint Petersburg Conservatory and leader of its Orchestra Class, while remaining in naval service and teaching in uniform. His fellow nationalists scorned the contrapuntal studies he took up, and Borodin described his Third Symphony as the work of a German professor with his glasses on. He later became civilian Inspector of Naval Bands, which taught him woodwind and brass playing. In 1882 he met the patron Mitrofan Belyayev. In March 1889 he heard Wagner's Ring cycle in Saint Petersburg, was astonished by Wagner's mastery of orchestration and thereafter devoted himself almost exclusively to opera.</p><p>He completed Alexander Borodin's Prince Igor with Alexander Glazunov and orchestrated Alexander Dargomyzhsky's The Stone Guest three times, and his editing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modest-mussorgsky">Modest Mussorgsky</a>'s works remains controversial. His operas include The Maid of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pskov">Pskov</a>, May Night, The Snow Maiden, Sadko and The Golden Cockerel, and the Flight of the Bumblebee comes from The Tale of Tsar Saltan. His orchestral works drew on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> liturgical themes, folk song and orientalism, and he revised nearly all of them, some more than once.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Rimsky-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korsakov-sakhalin">Korsakov</a> suffered from angina from about 1890, and the strains of the 1905 Revolution, when the Conservatory saw student demonstrations, hastened its course. He died in 1908 at the age of 64 on his Lubensk estate near Luga and was buried at the Tikhvin Cemetery of the Alexander Nevsky Monastery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>, next to Borodin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-glinka">Mikhail Glinka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modest-mussorgsky">Mussorgsky</a>. Over 35 years he taught 250 students at the Conservatory, among them Glazunov, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sergei-prokofiev">Sergei Prokofiev</a> and Ottorino Respighi, and he privately taught <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igor-stravinsky">Igor Stravinsky</a>. He was a transitional figure between the self-taught composers of The Five and the professionally trained composers who became the norm by the end of the 19th century. His Principles of Orchestration was completed posthumously by Maximilian Steinberg.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nikolai Rimsky-Korsakov” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Composers from Carl Maria von Weber to <b>Nikolai Rimsky-Korsakov</b>, whose Scheherazade dates from 1888, and Maurice Ravel set its stories, and illustrators from Gustave Doré to Edmund Dulac drew them.</blockquote><a href="/work/one-thousand-and-one-nights">One Thousand and One Nights · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Nikolai Rimsky-Korsakov</b> made the same mistake in his &quot;Neapolitan Song&quot; of 1907.</blockquote><a href="/songs/funiculi-funicula">Funiculì, Funiculà</a></li></ul></section>
